A||||14||XDrewX||POLL: How many people with V8's have a radiator other than the stock one.||||1||
Z||000000||XDrewX||12-11-2001||12:34 AM||||Please exuse the wording in the title.<BR>( I am falling asleep at the keyboard)<P>I would like to know how many people have a V8 and did not change the radiator from a v6.<P>If you did not change radiator, how is the stock one working?<P>Please include the atmospheric temperatures you commonly drive in.<P>Also If you used a 160 fan switch and thermostat, would that allow you to use the stock V6 radiator with a SBC.<P>Any opinions, advise, etc. would be appreciated.<P>Thanks<p>[This message has been edited by XDrewX (edited 12-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ferrari_cdn||12-11-2001||12:45 AM||||That was what I was wondering too.  I plan for a 4.9L caddy, and I have an Iron Duke.  So I don't know if I can use my stock radiator with the v8, or a different one.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ICEFierosig.gif"> AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||1
Z||000002||Banzai||12-11-2001||06:47 AM||||I haven't done the swap but I'm pretty sure you will have to get a new radiator. I was looking at the rad in my 84' and it looks like it could be recored(probobly less than $100). The four only has one row down the middle of the tanks, I've never seen a six rad, but I'm sure it not much bigger(thicker.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Hartz||12-11-2001||08:27 AM||||I have a Griffin, brass, 4 core radiator in mine.  Works like a champ, but it was a bit of a bodge to get it in there.  Archie sells a 4 core radiator that is a direct bolt in.  I'd look at those...  The stock radiator will be marginal at best.<P>Hartz<P>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo<BR>hartzfeld.home.mindspring.com||||reg||1
Z||000004||Daleford||12-11-2001||08:30 AM||||Although I am not [legally] on the road yet with a 4.9 Cadillac installed, I am running cool with a stock v-6 rad. I have the Fiero stock fan attached and it is doing it's thing.<BR>The temperature has not been above 65 however, since we finished the swap, so next summer will tell the tale. This is a Testarossa rebody project so I have lots of room for oil coolers with giant air intakes.  Will be experimenting with that before I ever buy a special rad. <P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||XDrewX||12-11-2001||10:00 AM||||I was thinking of trying an oil cooler, first before a new radiator.  I was thinking of putting it under the drivers side engine grill, and remove the louver psc. under that grill.<P>Thanks<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Butter||12-11-2001||10:48 AM||||XDrewX,<BR>I have run with a stock 84 radiator and I use a CSI Electric Waterpump.  As I remember I upgraded to the 4 flue Archie radiator because in temperatures above approximately 90 the temp hand would continue to climb slowly till overheating, if drove on the 4 lane at about 70 mph much farther than 20 miles.  This may vary with any particular setup as I was able to delay the overheating by restricting the heater core circulation.  The four flue radiator works well and I  have not had to worry with creeping temp hands. <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/buttersig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||California Kid||12-11-2001||10:59 AM||||4 Core Alum. Radiator (looks stock), 165 Thermal switch (with manual overide), no thermostat (restrictor flange at engine), Corvette short shaft water pump-belt driven.<P>No Thermal issues, have even run air conditioner in 95 degree heat in stop and go traffic.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/californiakidsig_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||wcapman||12-11-2001||04:47 PM||||I run a Superior 4 core copper radiator in my N* conversion.  I have no problems.  There are two fans, the stock is the main low speed fan and there are two 10" fans in front of the AC condenser that are the high speed/AC fans.  I live on the Gulf Coast where it's "HOT" and actually have a hard time making the car come up to temp.  This could have something to do with the aluminum engine but it likes to run around 195.  BTW, the N* thermostat is on the return line and the ECM controls the fans.  If I let it idle for 15 minutes the fan sometimes comes on.  I also have a Ferrari F355 nose, which lets much more air into the system, and a Scorpion hood, which lets the hot air out over the hood.   Okay, so maybe I'm not a good example...||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrfixit58||12-11-2001||05:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by XDrewX:<BR><B>I was thinking of trying an oil cooler, first before a new radiator.  I was thinking of putting it under the drivers side engine grill, and remove the louver psc. under that grill.<P>Thanks</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>A guy in my Fiero club did his SBC V8 that way. He said it made all the difference in the world even though he was using a special built 4-core radiator. He also instaled a fan under the core with a remote switch. He says he seldom needs to use it.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>Suncoast Fieros<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">          <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <P><B>JUST ADDED: </B>1988 GT w/ T-Tops, 5-Speed, and Performance Sound.||||reg||1
Z||000010||rockcrawl||12-11-2001||05:36 PM||||I'm using a stock v6 radiator with a 4.5 liter V8. I ran it in 90+ degree temps this past summer and the coolant stayed at a steady 175, city or highway. But this is only a 200 hp engine. There are lots of different Fiero radiators. Different core widths and fin counts. The V6 radiator has a 50% thicker core than the 4cyl, but the 4cyl with heavy duty cool option also has the thicker radiator. The air velocity going through the radiator determines the best number of fins per inch, and varies depending on which nose is on the car. Apparently fin count has a large effect on cooling.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP01288.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Tina||12-11-2001||05:55 PM||||Custom 4 -core here<P>Tina  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||mafv8||12-11-2001||07:06 PM||||I have an Archie 4 core on mine, no thermostat, and had no problems, even when using the air.||||reg||1
Z||000013||JD86GT350||12-11-2001||07:31 PM||||Archie 4 row here, if <B>the man</B> recommends it, it's probably a good idea||||reg||1
Z||000014||jelly2m8||12-11-2001||08:34 PM||||Im running the stock V6 rad in my TPI V8 with a 195* thermostat.<P>Temperature ranges from ~ -15*C to +30*C year round.<P>Engine runs at the optimum 195-198*F<BR>(Snap-On scantool).<P> Might be a different story farther south, where it gets really warm.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
